<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for 
<br class="ltx_break">Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jie Liu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiayi Shen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Pan Zhou
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding authors.</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jan-Jakob Sonke
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Amsterdam   Singapore Management University   The Netherlands Cancer Institute
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Efstratios Gavves
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Generalized Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation (GFSS) aims to extend a segmentation model to novel classes with only a few annotated examples while maintaining performance on base classes.
Recently, pretrained vision-language models (VLMs) such as CLIP have been leveraged in GFSS to improve generalization on novel classes through multi-modal prototypes learning.
However, existing prototype-based methods are inherently deterministic, limiting the adaptability of learned prototypes to diverse samples, particularly for novel classes with scarce annotations.
To address this, we propose FewCLIP, a probabilistic prototype calibration framework over multi-modal prototypes from the pretrained CLIP, thus providing more adaptive prototype learning for GFSS.
Specifically, FewCLIP first introduces a prototype calibration mechanism, which refines frozen textual prototypes with learnable visual calibration prototypes, leading to a more discriminative and adaptive representation.
Furthermore, unlike deterministic prototype learning techniques, FewCLIP introduces distribution regularization over these calibration prototypes.
This probabilistic formulation ensures structured and uncertainty-aware prototype learning, effectively mitigating overfitting to limited novel class data while enhancing generalization.
Extensive experimental results on PASCAL-5<sup id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="abstract1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> and COCO-20<sup id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="abstract1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> datasets demonstrate that our proposed FewCLIP significantly outperforms state-of-the-art approaches across both GFSS and class-incremental setting.
The code is available at <a href="https://github.com/jliu4ai/FewCLIP" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/jliu4ai/FewCLIP</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> aims to assign semantic labels to each pixel of an image and has achieved remarkable success in the deep learning era. However, traditional semantic segmentation heavily relies on vast amounts of data with pixel-wise annotations and often struggles with classes absent from the training set, a.k.a. novel classes. To tackle these challenges, few-shot semantic segmentation (FSS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> has emerged. FSS first trains a base model on many annotated data of base classes to learn general knowledge, and then fine-tunes the model on a few annotated samples of novel classes for adaptation. However, FSS primarily focuses on the binary segmentation of novel classes at test time, but lacks identification ability on base classes and multi-novel classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22979v1/motivation.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/423;" width="685" height="423" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Concept diagram comparing deterministic and probabilistic prototype learning with pre-trained CLIP in GFSS task.<span id="S1.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (a) Existing methods integrate a learnable or precomputed visual prototype with a frozen textual prototype for each class to perform pixel-wise deterministic classification.
(b) Our proposed FewCLIP models the class-wise visual calibration prototype as a distribution, capturing intra-class variations and uncertainty. By representing prototypes with a probabilistic distribution, our method mitigates prototype overfitting to scarce data, thereby improving generalization to novel classes.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a more practical and ambitious extension, generalized few-shot semantic segmentation (GFSS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> aims to enable the segmentation of both base and novel classes within a given image.
Typically, GFSS consists of two phases: (1) the <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">base class training phase</span>, where a base segmentation model is trained on base classes with abundant annotated samples, and (2) the <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">novel class registration phase</span>, where novel classes are integrated into the model using only a few annotated samples per class.
Due to the significant data availability gap between these two phases, the primary challenge in GFSS lies in <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">enhancing model generalization to novel classes while preserving performance on base classes</em>.
Generally, existing methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> tackle this challenge by decoupling the prototype learning of base and novel classes, ensuring that only novel class prototypes are optimized during the novel class registration phase.
More recently, O3S <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> introduced multi-modal prototypes learning with pre-trained CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> to further improve model generalization on novel classes.
However, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (a), existing prototype-based methods remain deterministic, failing to account for the inherent uncertainty in learned visual prototypes.
For instance, images from the same class can exhibit significant variations in appearance, pose, and orientation, making it challenging for a deterministic prototype to adequately capture all possible variations.
As a result, overlooking uncertainties in prototype learning can lead to overfitting on the limited training samples and causing the model to forget generalizable knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
This issue is particularly severe during the novel class registration phase, where visual prototypes must be learned from only a few annotated samples, further hindering model generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> To address these limitations, we propose FewCLIP, a novel probabilistic prototype calibration framework that integrates uncertainty-aware prototype learning with pretrained vision-language models (VLMs).
To enhance generalization to novel classes, we first introduce a prototype calibration mechanism, where FewCLIP calibrates frozen textual prototypes from pre-trained CLIP with learnable visual calibration prototypes in a class-specific manner.
This mechanism preserves the generalizable knowledge from CLIP while incorporating class-specific information by learning calibration prototypes from supervised data.
By optimizing only the visual prototypes for novel classes during the novel class registration phase, FewCLIP ensures effective adaptation to novel classes while preserving base class knowledge.
Furthermore, to mitigate the overfitting of calibration prototypes on limited observed samples—especially in the novel class registration phase, we introduce probabilistic prototype calibration.
As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (b), instead of representing calibration prototypes as deterministic vectors, we model them as continuous probability distributions, allowing them to capture uncertainty and intra-class variation in the learned representations.
This probabilistic formulation provides a distribution regularization over visual calibration prototypes, mitigating overfitting to limited novel class samples, thereby improving model generalization over these classes.
In summary, our contributions are threefold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> We introduce a prototype calibration mechanism for GFSS, adapting frozen textual prototypes with visual calibration prototypes to enhance model generalization to novel classes while maintaining performance on base classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> We propose FewCLIP, a probabilistic prototype calibration framework modeling calibration prototypes as probability distributions to mitigate prototype overfitting and enable uncertainty-aware prototype learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> Extensive experiments on PASCAL-5<sup id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> and COCO-20<sup id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S1.p6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> benchmarks demonstrate the superiority of our method in both GFSS and class-incremental FSS settings, achieving around 10% mIoU improvement on novel classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation (GFSS).</span> Unlike conventional few-shot semantic segmentation, GFSS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> aims to recognize both base and novel classes in an image, leveraging sufficient data from base classes and a few data from novel classes.
One line of research in GFSS seeks to <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mimic</span> the novel class registration during base class training, employing techniques such as contextual aggregation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, orthogonality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and kernel-based learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.
These methods enable a seamless extension of the label space when incorporating novel classes.
Another research direction explores various fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> strategy during novel class registration to effectively adapt models to novel classes.
More recently, BCM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> offer a strong baseline for GFSS by training novel-class models by supervised learning.
O3S <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> and BCM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> investigate how multi-modal knowledge from vision-language models can enhance model generalization to novel classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vision-Language Models (VLMs).</span>
Pre-trained Vision-Language Models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> connect the image representation with text embedding and have made rapid progress in recent years. Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training (CLIP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> is one of the most popular VLMs, by aligning the representation of two modalities in the same embedding space via contrastive learning.
In practice, CLIP has demonstrated strong zero-shot generalization across various downstream tasks, including visual question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, image retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and dense prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>.
Recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> have also explored leveraging CLIP’s cross-modal knowledge to improve GFSS. However, without task-specific fine-tuning, its performance on dense prediction tasks remains limited.
In this work, we investigate effective task-specific finetuning strategies for CLIP to address challenges in GFSS.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22979v1/main.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/242;" width="651" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;"> Overview of the proposed FewCLIP with probabilistic prototype calibration.<span id="S2.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Given an image and a set of class names, we first extract textual prototypes <math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and visual embeddings [<math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,H</annotation></semantics></math>] using CLIP’s text and image encoders, respectively.
For each class, we learn a sample-specific distribution <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z_{i}|P_{t}^{i},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z_{i}|P_{t}^{i},g)</annotation></semantics></math> and a calibration prototype <math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are further integrated into a probabilistic calibration prototype <math id="S2.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, the probabilistic calibration prototype <math id="S2.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to calibrate the textual prototype <math id="S2.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, the calibrated prototype <math id="S2.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and patch embeddings <math id="S2.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are fed into a mask decoder to generate the final mask prediction.
Novel classes can be seamlessly integrated into the model by fine-tuning a calibration prototype <math id="S2.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with few annotated data in the novel class registration phase.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameter Efficient Fine-Tuning of VLMs.</span>
Without task-specific fine-tuning, VLMs often perform poorly on downstream out-of-domain data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. For such case, parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods have been developed. One approach is to learn lightweight feature adapter modules for the textual and/or visual pathways <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. Another strategy is soft prompt tuning, which introduces a small set of continuous tokens as inputs to the frozen visual and/or textual encoders to capture task-specific information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
To enhance fine-tuning with uncertainty modeling in downstream tasks, some methods adopt probabilistic fine-tuning techniques, including variational prompt tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, probabilistic prompt learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and continual learning with probabilistic fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
In this work, we introduce a probabilistic prototype calibration mechanism for VLMs to improve GFSS performance on novel classes without compromising base class.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalized Few-shot Segmentation (GFSS).</span>
GFSS aims to extend the label space of a pre-trained segmentation model to novel classes using only a few annotated training samples while maintaining strong performance on base classes. Typically, GFSS follows a two-phase training scheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> In the <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">base class training phase</em>, a base segmentation model is trained on the base classes <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{b}=\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N^{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{b}=\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N^{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of base classes. Each base class has a sufficient number of annotated training images, enabling the model to learn general and transferable representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet</annotation></semantics></math> In the <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">novel class registration phase</em>, novel classes <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{n}=\{c_{i}\}_{i=N^{b}+1}^{N^{b}+N^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{n}=\{c_{i}\}_{i=N^{b}+1}^{N^{b}+N^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> are incorporated to the pre-trained base model, where <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of novel classes. This is typically achieved through fine-tuning, as each novel class has only a few annotated training samples, e.g., <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the <em id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inference phase</em>, given a test image, the trained model performs semantic segmentation by classifying each pixel into one of the <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+N^{b}+N^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+N^{b}+N^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> classes, including <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> base classes, <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> novel classes, and a background class.
However, existing GFSS methods suffer from poor performance on either novel classes, as seen in prototype-based fine-tuning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, or base classes, as in adapter-based fine-tuning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.
Therefore, the key challenge in GFSS is to <em id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">improving the generalization on novel classes</em> with limited annotated data while <em id="S3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">maintaining performance on base classes</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP Fine-tuning for Segmentation Tasks.</span>
Typically, recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> freeze the text and image encoders of pre-trained CLIP in the segmentation tasks.
To effectively adapt CLIP for segmentation, these works incorporate visual prompt tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> into the image encoder and learn a mask decoder to enhance text-image matching.
Specifically, given <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> class names <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{c_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we encode them using the CLIP text encoder <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the corresponding set of textual prototype representations, denoted as <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}=\{P_{t}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}=\{P_{t}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each prototype is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{i}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{text}}(c_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{i}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{text}}(c_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Similarly, for a given image <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract its visual representation using the frozen image encoder <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{image}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>image</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{image}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[g,H]=\mathcal{F}_{\text{image}}([X,V_{p}])," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>image</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[g,H]=\mathcal{F}_{\text{image}}([X,V_{p}]),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the class token embedding with size <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=\{h_{i}\}_{i=1}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=\{h_{i}\}_{i=1}^{L}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> local visual feature patch <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a set of deep visual prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, which introduce task-specific learnable parameters into the input space.
Then, the segmentation mask is computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{mask}}(\psi(P_{t}),\Psi(H))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}=\mathcal{F}_{\text{mask}}(\psi(P_{t}),\Psi(H)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{mask}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable mask decoder deployed as a lightweight transformer. <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi</annotation></semantics></math> denote the projection layers on the prototypes and feature patches, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Overview</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the overview of our proposed FewCLIP with probabilistic prototype calibration for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation (GFSS).
Given an image <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of class names <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, our goal is to accurately segment images from both base and novel classes using the general knowledge in Vision-Language Models (VLMs).
First, the image encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{image}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>image</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{image}}</annotation></semantics></math> and text encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{text}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{text}}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively extract visual embeddings [<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,H</annotation></semantics></math>] and textual prototypes <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively the class token and patch tokens.
Next, the class token <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and textual prototypes <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are fed into a probabilistic multi-modal encoder to generate a sample-specific distribution for each class, e.g., <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)</annotation></semantics></math> for base class <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The sampled representations from this distribution are then fused with a learnable calibration prototype <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the probabilistic calibration prototype <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{c}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{c}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is further used to calibrate corresponding textual prototype <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Novel classes can be seamlessly integrated into the model by learning a calibration prototype for each class during novel class registration.
Finally, the calibrated multi-modal prototype <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> from both base and novel classes, along with the patch embeddings <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, are fed into a mask decoder <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{mask}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{mask}}</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the mask <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Prototype Calibration</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the pre-trained CLIP model, our goal is to learn a compact multi-modal prototype for each base and novel class, enabling accurate segmentation of both during inference.
While textual prototypes derived from class names provide strong generalizable information, but textual prototypes alone lack fine-grained visual semantics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>, often leading to suboptimal segmentation performance.
To mitigate this issue, we introduce a set of learnable calibration prototypes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}=\{P_{c}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}=\{P_{c}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> to calibrate the frozen textual prototypes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}=\{P_{t}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}=\{P_{t}^{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> by incorporating class-specific visual information from observed annotated samples.
Here, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> dynamically changes according to the training stage of GFSS, i.e., <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=N^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=N^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> during the base class training phase and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=N^{b}+N^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=N^{b}+N^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> during the novel class registration phase.
Formally, the prototype calibration is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}=P_{t}\odot P_{c}+P_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}=P_{t}\odot P_{c}+P_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Hadamard product and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of calibrated multi-modal prototypes. The term <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}\odot P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}\odot P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> provides a multi-modal corrective bias to the frozen textual prototypes, yielding a more compact and generalizable multi-modal prototype. Experimental results in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.1 Comparison with state-of-the-art ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> of Sec. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> show superiority of the calibration format (<a href="#S4.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 4.2 Prototype Calibration ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Next, we concatenate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> with the background prototype <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the final calibrated prototypes <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{m}=\phi_{\text{proj}}([P_{0},P_{m}])\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\times d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{m}=\phi_{\text{proj}}([P_{0},P_{m}])\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\text{proj}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext>proj</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\text{proj}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a two-layered MLPs. The background prototype <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is learnable in the base class training phase but remain frozen in the novel class registration phase.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This decoupled calibration allows our model to retain CLIP’s generalization capacity within textual prototypes while acquiring class-specific visual semantics through calibration prototypes. During novel class registration, we freeze all parameters from the base class training phase, including base class calibration prototypes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and only fine-tune the novel class prototypes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{\text{novel}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>novel</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{\text{novel}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This seamlessly integrates novel classes without affecting base class representations. By replacing <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we obtain the predicted segmentation mask <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, our decoupled calibration enhances generalization on novel classes while preserving strong performance on base classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Probabilistic Prototype Calibration</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The novel class registration phase in GFSS suffers from data scarcity, making it challenging to learn a deterministic and representative calibration prototype for each novel class.
On one hand, the limited number of available samples can lead to overfitting, resulting in poor generalization to unseen data.
On the other hand, high intra-class variance—arising from differences in appearance, shape, and texture—necessitates a prototype that can explicitly capture uncertainty in its representation.
To address these challenges, we introduce probabilistic prototype calibration to model the calibration prototype as a probability distribution rather than a deterministic prototype vector.
This probabilistic formulation can better capture intra-class variations while mitigating overfitting in the few-shot setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probabilistic Modeling.</span>
For each class <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define a sample-specific probability distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on the class-specific textual prototype <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and sample-specific class token <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from the input image.
Here we assume that <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)</annotation></semantics></math> follows a Gaussian with a mean vector <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\mu}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\mu}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a variance vector <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{\mu}_{i},\mathbf{\sigma}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|P_{t}^{i},g)\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{\mu}_{i},\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we design a probabilistic multi-modal encoder to infer the parameters of this distribution.
Specifically, we employ a multi-head cross-attention layer (<span id="S4.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">MHCA</span>) to process the textual prototype <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and the visual class token <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling effective cross-modal feature fusion.
Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the query, while both key and value vectors are derived from <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
The mean and variance vectors are then estimated via two separate two-layered MLPs <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mu}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\mu}_{i}=\phi_{\mu}(\texttt{MHCA}(P_{t}^{i},g)),\quad\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}=\phi_{\sigma}(\texttt{MHCA}(P_{t}^{i},g))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MHCA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MHCA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\mu}_{i}=\phi_{\mu}(\texttt{MHCA}(P_{t}^{i},g)),\quad\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}=\phi_{\sigma}(\texttt{MHCA}(P_{t}^{i},g)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">To further enhance adaptability, we model the calibration prototype distribution across all classes as a Mixture of Gaussian distributions (MoG):</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|P_{t},g)\sim\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{\mu}_{i},\mathbf{\sigma}_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∼</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|P_{t},g)\sim\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{\mu}_{i},\mathbf{\sigma}_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This formulation enables diverse prototype sampling, enhancing adaptability to novel classes while preserving class consistency for robust segmentation.
Given <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|P_{t},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|P_{t},g)</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> latent prototypes for each class, namely, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\{z^{1},\cdots,z^{M}\}\sim p(z|P_{t},g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>M</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\{z^{1},\cdots,z^{M}\}\sim p(z|P_{t},g)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This sampling follows the reparameterization trick <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, ensuring differentiability for efficient optimization.
Accordingly, these sampled calibration prototypes represent learned distributions over class representations, capturing the inherent variability in visual appearance within each class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Probabilistic Calibration.</span>
Given the sampled calibration prototypes <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the final calibration prototype by combining them with the learnable calibration prototype <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{c}=\{P_{c}+z^{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{c}=\{P_{c}+z^{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This formulation incorporates both deterministic components from learnable calibration prototype <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and probabilistic variations from the sampled latent prototypes <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>.
The deterministic part captures visual semantics from the limited annotated samples, while the probabilistic component <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> acts as a regularization mechanism, preventing overfitting and improving generalization to novel instances.
By substituting the <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 4.2 Prototype Calibration ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{P}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>P</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{P}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform probabilistic prototype calibration and segmentation, yielding <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> mask predictions.
Finally, we adopt pixel-wise mean of these predictions as final mask, while the pixel-wise variance quantifies the model’s epistemic uncertainty, offering a measure of confidence in the segmentation results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Training Objective</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our training objective consists of two parts, a cross-entropy loss that supervises pixel-wise segmentation accuracy, and a KL divergence regularization term that aligns the distribution of calibration prototypes with a Gaussian prior <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring smooth and well-structured prototype learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Formally, the overall loss is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(\hat{Y},Y)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(p(z|P_{t},g)||\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CE</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{CE}}(\hat{Y},Y)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL}}(p(z|P_{t},g)||\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\mathbf{I})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a balancing weight and set as 0.001 during training.
During base class training, we freeze the image and text encoders of the pre-trained CLIP model, optimizing the visual prompts <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probabilistic multi-modal encoder, the calibration prototypes, and the mask decoder to adapt CLIP to the segmentation task.
In the novel class registration phase, we freeze all model parameters and only update the calibration prototypes <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> for novel classes, allowing the model to accommodate new categories without affecting previously learned base class representations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Extension to class incremental setting</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further extend our model to the class-incremental setting, a.k.a., Class-Incremental Few-Shot Semantic Segmentation (CIFSS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, where novel classes are introduced sequentially over multiple sessions. In CIFSS, training begins with a base session <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{b}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has sufficient labeled data. This is followed by a sequence of novel sessions <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}=\{S_{n}^{1},\cdots,S_{n}^{T}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}=\{S_{n}^{1},\cdots,S_{n}^{T}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, each containing a limited number of annotated samples. Importantly, at any given time step <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we only have access to the data from session <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is no overlap between novel sessions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our model naturally extends to this setting by incrementally updating the text and calibration prototypes. Formally, let <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the text prototype and calibration prototype of session <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. When transitioning to the next session <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s+1</annotation></semantics></math>, we update these prototypes as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{t}^{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{t}^{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[P_{t}^{s},P_{t}^{s+1}]\in\mathbb{R}^{N^{s+1}\times d}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[P_{t}^{s},P_{t}^{s+1}]\in\mathbb{R}^{N^{s+1}\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P_{c}^{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P_{c}^{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=[P_{c}^{s},P_{c}^{s+1}]\in\mathbb{R}^{N^{s+1}\times d}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=[P_{c}^{s},P_{c}^{s+1}]\in\mathbb{R}^{N^{s+1}\times d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where [<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>,<math id="S4.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>] is the concatenation operation, and <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes in the session <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s+1</annotation></semantics></math>. During incremental learning, we exclusively register novel classes by optimizing the calibration prototypes <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}^{s+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}^{s+1}</annotation></semantics></math> using limited data from these novel classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We test our method on two GFSS benchmarks: PASCAL-5<sup id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and COCO-20<sup id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
PASCAL-5<sup id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup>, a subset of PASCAL VOC 2012 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>,
consists of 12,301 images with high-quality, pixel-wise annotation across 20 semantic categories, divided into 4 splits for cross-validation (5 classes per split). COCO-20<sup id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup>, a subset of MSCOCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, has 122,218 images annotated across 80 object classes which are divided into 4 splits for cross-validation (20 classes per split) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. In the experiment, classes from a specific split are set novel classes, while the remaining are base classes. For CIFSS, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and adopt the same fold division with GFSS. Novel classes on both PASCAL-5<sup id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> and COCO-20<sup id="S5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.p1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> are divided into 5 incremental sessions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation metrics.</span>
We use the mean intersection-over-union (mIoU) as the performance metric. Specifically, we report mIoU for base and novel classes as <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_sub">B</sub></span> and <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_sub">N</sub></span>, respectively. We also report the overall mIoU on all classes, i.e., <span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.4.1" class="ltx_sub">O</sub></span>. As base classes is the majority, mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.5" class="ltx_sub">O</sub> could predominantly reflect mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.6" class="ltx_sub">B</sub> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
So we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and adopt the harmonic mean of mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.7" class="ltx_sub">B</sub> and mIoU<sub id="S5.p2.1.8" class="ltx_sub">N</sub>, denoted as <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IoU</span>, as the primary metric.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:144%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:63%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:63%;">Quantitative results under 1-shot and 5-shot GFSS settings on PASCAL-5<sup id="S5.T1.12.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T1.12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> and COCO-20<sup id="S5.T1.12.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T1.12.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup><span id="S5.T1.12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. <math id="S5.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote image and text are provided for novel class registration, respectively. The harmonic mean <math id="S5.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{h}\text{IoU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>IoU</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{h}\text{IoU}</annotation></semantics></math> is employed as the primary metric to balance the significance of the results for base classes, <math id="S5.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IoU</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> and novel class, <math id="S5.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IoU</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Best-performing results are highlighted in bold. Our method FewCLIP demonstrates notable improvements, particularly in the 1-shot setting.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.13" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:70.1pt;vertical-align:-33.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-367.5pt,51.8pt) scale(0.403370853884161,0.403370853884161) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.13.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.13.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
