<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CIFSS setting.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Comparison with state-of-the-art ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Probabilistic Prototype Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Generalized Few-shot Semantic Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the quantitative results for the CIFSS setting on the PASCAL-5<sup id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> and COCO-20<sup id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup>. Overall, our method exhibits significantly better performance than existing approaches in all three metrics, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IoU</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mtext>IoU</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{m}\text{IoU}_{\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{h}\text{IoU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>IoU</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{h}\text{IoU}</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, in session 1, our method exceeds the PKL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> by 15.42<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> and 21.32<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emph{h}\text{IoU}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>IoU</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emph{h}\text{IoU}</annotation></semantics></math> on PASCAL-5<sup id="S5.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup> and COCO-20<sup id="S5.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i</span></sup>, respectively. As the novel class session increases, the superiority of our method in handling both base and novel classes becomes even more pronounced. The results generally embodies the effectiveness of our model in dynamically accommodating novel classes in a session stream.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:144%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T4.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:63%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.T4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:63%;">Effects of prototype calibration formats in class-specific calibration<span id="S5.T4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. <math id="S5.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> denote frozen textual embeddings and learnable corrective prototypes. [<math id="S5.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>] stands for concatenation.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T4.12" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:506.8pt;height:133pt;vertical-align:-62.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(41.1pt,-10.8pt) scale(1.19381855564821,1.19381855564821) ;">
